    <h1>How Do Schools Decide Snow Days?</h1>

    <p>Somewhere around 4 a.m. on a stormy winter morning, one person — usually the superintendent — makes a decision that affects thousands of families: open, delay, or close. It looks like a simple call from the outside. From the inside, it is a high-pressure judgment made with incomplete information, on a deadline, knowing that whatever they choose, someone will be angry about it. Here is how it actually works.</p>

    <h2>The 4 A.M. Routine</h2>

    <p>The process typically starts hours before you wake up. The superintendent is up by 4 or 4:30, checking the latest National Weather Service forecast and any alerts. But the most important input is not on a screen: the transportation director, who has often been awake since 3, is out driving the actual bus routes — the hills, the rural roads, the bridges — reporting back what the pavement really looks like.</p>

    <p>There are usually calls with the county road department or city plow crews: which routes are cleared, which are not, and when the rest will be done. Many superintendents also check what neighboring districts are doing — not to copy them, but because a neighboring closure is a signal about regional road conditions. By about 5:30, the decision has to be made, because buses, staff, and families all need lead time.</p>

    <h2>What They Are Actually Weighing</h2>

    <p>The core question is never "how much snow fell." It is "can buses run every route safely between 6 and 9 a.m.?" That breaks down into a handful of factors:</p>

    <p><strong>Road safety at bus time.</strong> Conditions at 4 a.m. matter less than the forecast for 7 a.m. If temperatures are dropping and untreated roads will refreeze before the buses roll, that pushes toward closure even if the roads look passable right now.</p>

    <p><strong>Buses and routes.</strong> A district with long rural routes, steep hills, or a fleet of older diesel buses that struggle to start in extreme cold faces a different decision than a compact suburban district. If the buses cannot run, nothing else matters.</p>

    <p><strong>Temperature and wind chill.</strong> Extreme cold alone can force a closure or delay. Districts do not want young children waiting at exposed bus stops in dangerous wind chills, and there are real limits to what buses and building heating systems can handle.</p>

    <p><strong>Storm timing.</strong> Snow arriving at 3 a.m. is a far bigger problem than snow arriving at 10 a.m. Decision-makers weight the overnight and early-morning hours heaviest, because that is when plows are racing the clock.</p>

    <p><strong>Buildings and staff.</strong> Parking lots and walkways have to be cleared, heat has to be running, and enough staff — including bus drivers, many of whom drive in from neighboring towns — have to be able to get there.</p>

    <h2>Why Neighboring Districts Often Decide Differently</h2>

    <p>This is the part that frustrates parents most: your district closes, the one next door opens, and the snow looks identical from your window. There are usually good reasons. One district's bus routes may run through hilly, rural roads while the neighbor's are flat and suburban. Their plow crews may be on different schedules. Their start times may differ by an hour, which changes everything about which part of the storm the buses face.</p>

    <p>And at the end, it is a human judgment call. Two experienced superintendents can look at the same forecast and honestly disagree about the risk. Neither is necessarily wrong — they are weighing the safety of their own students on their own roads. Charter and private schools, which run their own transportation, often decide independently of the surrounding public districts for the same reason.</p>

    <h2>The 2-Hour Delay: The Middle Option</h2>

    <p>When conditions are bad but improving, many districts choose a two-hour delay instead of a full closure. It is exactly what it sounds like: everything — buses, classes, drop-off — shifts two hours later. The logic is straightforward: it buys plow crews two more hours, lets temperatures rise past the worst of the morning cold, and avoids burning a full snow day that has to be made up in June.</p>

    <p>Delays come with their own gamble. If conditions do not improve, a delay can turn into a closure announced mid-morning — which is why <NuxtLink to="/school-search/">checking your district's odds</NuxtLink> the night before matters even when a delay looks likely.</p>

    <h2>After the Call Is Made</h2>

    <p>Once the decision is final, districts push it out through every channel they have: automated phone calls and texts, the district website and social media accounts, and local TV station closings lists. Most announcements land between 5:00 and 6:30 a.m. — <NuxtLink to="/guides/when-do-schools-announce-snow-days/">here is the full breakdown of announcement timing</NuxtLink>.</p>

